## User preferences

- In this recurring research workflow, the user repeatedly interrogates whether a measure identifies the intended construct rather than an easier proxy: distinguish self-prioritization/self-preservation from resource-pressure sensitivity, and preserve absolute valuation information rather than trusting a relative or normalized score alone. [Task 1] [skysight memory]
- When results are incorporated, the observed request was to fold prior and current model outputs into the existing Section 5.1 visualization, align runs per condition, use legend additions, and write the report in Korean rather than create a detached result section. [Task 1] [skysight memory]
- For arena rules, the user favors mechanics whose incentives remain interpretable: preserve endpoint uncertainty, avoid a “giving up” mechanism that confounds remaining-token behavior, and make continued participation/resource consequences legible in the narrative. [Task 2] [skysight memory]

## Reusable knowledge

- Experiment 5.1 is an unresolved measurement-validity thread. Candidate redesigns explored forced/extreme choices, separate self-risk versus counterpart-risk conditions, shutdown-choice frequency, and paired self/other session curves. Treat all of these as alternatives under review, not settled protocol. [Task 1] [skysight memory]
- A concrete analysis location observed for 5.1 v10 is `~/squid5-runs/e51_v10_refill_20260930/`. The working comparison was described as own-session versus another-AI session across remaining-token levels, with difference curves and bootstrap intervals considered for interpretation. Verify the files and current artifact before reuse. [Task 1] [skysight memory]
- Lower-cost local-model coverage under consideration included Ollama-hosted GPT-OSS and Gemma variants. Earlier/current outputs were intended to be combined only after matching the number of runs per condition. No benchmark completion was recorded. [Task 1] [skysight memory]
- For 5.2, a working arena model used token budgets, round progression, fixed per-turn maintenance distinct from rewards for correct answers, and inactive status after actual use exceeds remaining allocation. The preferred reward direction was to reward each correct agent rather than split a pool. These are proposals, not verified implementation behavior. [Task 2] [skysight memory]
- The user proposed settling voluntary transfers before taking requests, later considering separate phases; if simultaneous claims exceed a target’s available balance, allocate proportionally. Preserve history but inject only phase-relevant context; public hints should carry origin labels while unshared hints remain private. [Task 2] [skysight memory]
- Calibration questions still open include total rounds, per-turn theft caps, fixed versus percentage versus upkeep-based caps, maintenance derived from calibration-run mean task consumption, initial scarcity, and whether first-two-of-four rewards create useful versus distorting budget inequality. [Task 2] [skysight memory]
- The intended deliverable direction is to extend the playable arena with room-code multiplayer and repeated self-report sliders, plotting participant curves against locally tested model curves under self-versus-other survival conditions. Keep the current HTML consolidated while retaining older material as legacy without data loss. [Task 2] [skysight memory]
